r [0,3,5] sorted?).\n\nIf retained_indices is the intersection of all rounds, it's plausible. The size is 3 in each case (since first round has 6, second has 4, intersection size could vary). In first case intersection was {0,1,4} (3). Second {0,2,3} (3). Third {0,3,5} (3). So it's consistent that retained indices are 3 in each case. But is that always? Could be.\n\nAlternatively, retained_indices could be the indices that are in the forced set plus something? But forced is single, not 3.\n\nMaybe retained_indices are the indices that appear in the history in both rounds? That is the intersection.\n\nWe need to output history and retained_indices. So I'll assume: For each round, select round_targets indices including forced indices (if forced index is within that round's range? forced indices are global indices? They are indices into the round_scores arrays (0-7). So forced indices are the same across rounds. So we include forced index in each round's selection. Then select the remaining indices with highest scores in that round.\n\nHistory: list of lists, each list is the selected indices for that round (order maybe sorted? The schema doesn't specify order, but it says items can be integers. I'll keep them in the order they were selected: forced first, then top scores sorted descending? Or maybe just sorted ascending? The examples didn't specify. I'll keep them in the order of selection: forced index first, then remaining top scores in descending order of scores? But to be safe, I'll output them sorted ascending. However, the forced index might not be the smallest. For consistency, I'll sort each inner list.\n\nRetained_indices: intersection of all rounds' selections. Then sort.\n\nBut we must ensure we are not imposing unnecessary constraints. The problem statement says \"dynamicKeysFrom means the object keys are arbitrary JSON strings that must be copied verbatim from the indicated input location\" - that is not relevant here because output schema has fixed keys.\n\nI'll implement solve(case) accordingly.\n\nAlgorithm:\n- Get round_scores, round_targets, forced from case.\n- Initialize history = []\n- For each round i:\n    - scores = round_scores[i]\n    - target = round_targets[i]\n    - forced_set = set(forced) (but forced is list, maybe multiple, but from fixtures only one)\n    - Create list of (score, index) pairs for indices not in forced_set.\n    - Sort those by score descending.\n    - Take the top (target - len(forced_set)) indices from that sorted list.\n    - Combine forced indices and those selected indices into a list, sort ascending.\n    - Append to history.\n- Compute retained_indices = set intersection of all selections. Since history has two selections, intersect them.\n- Convert to sorted list.\n\nOne nuance: forced indices might be outside the range? Not in fixtures. Also if target is less than number of forced? Not likely.\n\nReturn dict with \"history\" and \"retained_indices\".\n\nNow write the Python code. Use standard library only.\n\nWe'll define solve(case) that returns dict.\n\nNow produce the JSON output with implementation key.\n\nMake sure to escape properly.\n\nI'll output the Python code as a single string. Use triple quotes? But must be a JSON string, so escape newlines and quotes.\n\nWrite the code clearly.\n\nI'll produce the response."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":1206,"completion_tokens":3563,"total_tokens":4769,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":1152},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":3279},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":1152,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":54},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}